# Herdr rules — the observable substrate

> **Secondary substrate.** The default in this method is programmatic fan-out: a script
> spawns workers, collects returns, and the orchestrator never sees the middle. Herdr is the
> alternative — real panes, named agents, observable lifecycle. Adopt it when the trade in
> [`_RULES.md`](_RULES.md) §2b says to, not by default.

Herdr organises terminals into workspaces, tabs and panes, recognises coding agents inside
panes, and exposes the session through the `herdr` CLI. Every rule below exists because the
substrate makes something *visible* that programmatic fan-out leaves *inferred*.

**The binary is the authority on syntax.** Run `herdr --help`, then a command group with no
subcommand (`herdr agent`, `herdr pane`, `herdr worktree`) to print its surface. Do not run bare
`herdr` to discover — it launches the TUI. Do not probe a mutating nested command by omitting
arguments; `herdr workspace create` is valid with defaults and will execute.

---

## 0 · Confirm you are inside it

```bash
test "${HERDR_ENV:-}" = 1
```

If that fails, you are not in a Herdr pane. Say so and stop — never inspect or drive a session
from outside it. When it passes, your own coordinates are injected:

```bash
printf '%s\n' "$HERDR_WORKSPACE_ID" "$HERDR_TAB_ID" "$HERDR_PANE_ID"
```

---

## 1 · Every agent gets a name — this is the whole point

`agent start` takes a name matching `[a-z][a-z0-9_-]{0,31}`, unique among live agents:

```bash
herdr agent start <role-name> --kind <kind> --pane <pane-id>
```

An unnamed fleet is addressable only by opaque pane ID, with terminal titles as the sole clue to
purpose. Five agents called `claude` is not a fleet, it is a coincidence. **Name by role, not by
model** — `reviewer`, `migrator`, `t14-search` — so the name still means something when the model
tier changes.

Names follow the pane occupant and clear when that agent exits, is released, or is replaced.
Agent commands accept a live name or the pane ID hosting it; they do not accept terminal IDs or
bare kind labels.

Existing unnamed agents can be adopted rather than restarted:

```bash
herdr agent rename <pane-id> <role-name>
```

---

## 2 · Read state; never infer it

| State | Means |
|---|---|
| `working` | actively producing |
| `idle` | ready for input, and its tab has been seen in the focused UI |
| `done` | the same idle state after *unseen* background work finished |
| `blocked` | Herdr recognised an approval or question UI |
| `unknown` | an agent is present but unclassified — **not** proof of completion |

```bash
herdr agent list                 # every agent, every workspace, one call
herdr agent get <target>
herdr agent read <target> --source recent-unwrapped --lines 120
```

`blocked` is the state programmatic fan-out cannot see. There, an agent waiting on an approval
prompt is indistinguishable from an agent thinking, until it times out. Here it is a queryable
fact — which is the single strongest argument for this substrate.

Treat `unknown` as unknown. It is not a success.

CLI reads do **not** mark a tab seen; only focusing it does. That is why `done` exists as a
distinct label from `idle`.

---

## 3 · Submitting work

```bash
herdr agent prompt <name> "<task>" --wait --timeout 120000
```

`agent prompt` atomically submits text and Enter, honouring the pane's live bracketed-paste mode.
`--wait` returns on the first settled `idle`, `done` or `blocked`. Do not restate those defaults
with `--until`.

A prompt sent from a non-working state must produce an observed lifecycle change within five
seconds, or Herdr returns `agent_prompt_stalled` rather than waiting forever. The wait tracks
lifecycle, not a specific turn — if the agent was already working, the active turn completing can
satisfy it.

Use `--until` only for a state-specific wait:

```bash
herdr agent wait <name> --until blocked --timeout 120000
```

When a wait returns `blocked`, **read before you send**: `agent get`, then `agent read`. Sending
keys at an approval prompt you have not read is how an agent approves something nobody chose.

```bash
herdr agent send-keys <name> esc
```

---

## 4 · Layout discipline

Default to a sibling pane in the current tab and the caller's working directory. Do not create a
workspace, tab or worktree unless the topology was actually asked for.

```bash
herdr pane layout --pane "$HERDR_PANE_ID"          # inspect before splitting
herdr pane split --current --direction right --cwd "$PWD" --no-focus
```

Split a wide pane **right**, a narrow or tall pane **down**. Avoid repeated same-direction splits
that produce unusable columns. Read the new pane ID from `.result.pane.pane_id` — parse it from
JSON, never derive it from sidebar order or an example.

**`--no-focus` on all background work.** The human's focus belongs to the human. Use `--current`
or an explicit ID; omitting a target may hit the UI-focused pane, which can belong to someone
else entirely.

`agent start` requires an existing shell pane at an interactive prompt with nothing in the
foreground. It never creates or moves layout. Use pane commands for ordinary processes and agent
commands only when identity and lifecycle matter.

---

## 5 · Isolation

Herdr's worktree commands do the phase-7 job and the observability job in one step:

```bash
herdr worktree create --branch <name> --base <ref> --label <text> --no-focus
```

This does not repeal the phase-7 rule. **Separate worktrees are the easy half of isolation; the
contended-file list is the hard half.** A branch per task still ends in one merge, and two agents
editing one file are still one conflict, relocated. Name the contended files and give each a
single owner regardless of substrate.

---

## 6 · Reading output

| Source | Use for |
|---|---|
| `visible` | the rendered viewport |
| `recent` | recent output including soft wraps |
| `recent-unwrapped` | **logs and transcripts** — soft wraps joined |
| `detection` | the plain-text snapshot Herdr classifies agents from |

Use `--format ansi` only when colour is evidence.

If raising `--lines` stops revealing more of a finished response, the agent is drawing on the
terminal's alternate screen; those rows never enter host scrollback and no line count recovers
them. **Fallback only then:** ask the agent to write its full response to a file and reply with
the path, then read the file. Do not ask for file output up front — it costs a round trip on
every task to solve a problem most tasks do not have.

---

## 7 · Do NOT

- Do NOT close a workspace, tab, pane or session you did not create.
- Do NOT run `herdr server stop` from an active session — it stops the server and its panes.
- Do NOT kill the main Herdr process. Use a named session for experiments needing isolation.
- Do NOT steal focus for background work.
- Do NOT target another client's focused pane by omitting a target.
- Do NOT treat `unknown` as done, or a read as proof a tab was seen.
- Do NOT reuse a pane ID after `pane move` — take the new one from
  `.result.move_result.pane.pane_id`. Closed IDs are never reused.

---

## 8 · Why this substrate survives amnesia

Principle 14 says assume amnesia: anything living only in a conversation is already gone.

Programmatic fan-out holds worker state in the orchestrator's context. Clear it and the fleet
becomes invisible — still running, unaddressable, its progress unknown until it returns or dies.

Herdr holds that state in the session. A cleared orchestrator runs one command:

```bash
herdr agent list
```

and recovers every agent, its pane, its cwd, and its lifecycle state. **The fleet outlives the
conversation that started it.** Named agents make that recovery legible rather than merely
possible — which is why §1 is a rule and not a nicety.
